 traits  on a different genotype if you can  imagine  joining two genotypes together  two apple genotypes you have  one that hit that has  certain characteristics in terms of  hormones and  gene expression  uh  also the way the tissue is arranged and  then it's joined with another genotype  that has completely or somewhat  different characteristics in terms of  how it perceives those hormones or  perceives water status or or something  else and so  whenever you make  one combination  and you measure  everything like yield and  precalcity or early bearing  in one combination  things may be slightly different when  you join  in with another  cyan variety so  something that is  good for fuji apples  may not be so good for another type of  apple like gala or honey crisp or  you know some of the new ones uh  snapdragon from  from uh  cornell  and uh and therefore we have to some  what account for these differences  it becomes  pretty complex  20 years ago up to 20 years ago we used  to have a monoculture pretty much a  monoculture of m9 root stocks that was  the  rootstock that was  uh applied in  about 90 percent of uh  newer dwarfing apple orchards in the u.s  we've been able to transition through uh  by releasing new geneva root stocks that  are disease resistant and  and uh more productive we've been able  to transition to  a much more diverse uh portfolio of  apple rootstocks  so we have about  12 apple root stocks that our program  released and they've all they all have  fairly different applications  some are more vigorous but yet  still  very  yield efficient  and  some are less vigorous or  promote a smaller tree  but are able to  produce larger fruit in certain cases we  have come up with a concept that's  called designer rootstock  or a rootstock that's designed  to fit in certain circumstances  for example  we use  we could use a very different rootstocks  here in new york compared to washington  state  and then compared to brazil where we  actually do have a good production of  our roost stocks and then compared to  south africa and california and  pennsylvania and maryland  why  well because  soil quality is different  or the soils are different the water  quality and the amount of water  available is also different  this orchard right here is not irrigated  and we it's completely reliant on  on natural  rain water whereas most of the orchards  out west in washington state idaho and  oregon all the way down to california  require irrigation  and so  that is also a  something that we have to account for  when we breed because  it's the root system that absorbs the  water the nutrients and everything else  and sends them up to the to the scion  and so where  how  that irrigation is done is influencing  the way a rootstock performs  so  with apple roots we're actually breeding  two  different crops  and  why do i say that  well  we're breeding to generate  these  little things these little sticks these  are actually root stocks  and so we have a  a point in the nursery where  we propagate these we can propagate by  micropropagation but also by  stooling and so the soothing properties  are also important so we breed for  that  part of the growth cycle where we have  mother plants that are cut down  every year to produce these liners  rootstock liners  and then we breed for the phase in the  orchard  so they they move from a rootstock  nursery to a finished tree nursery these  root stocks they get joined by grafting  and  budding and i'll demonstrate right that  right now we'll start with a rootstock  uh liner and this will be planted in the  ground and and this is the the ground  lining  a very expert person will  make a  incision like this in another incision  like this  hold on  and expose the uh  the cambium  okay  just like that  and then  they'll go back and they'll have a  bundle of uh bud sticks  and the buds have not quite  they're not quite completely developed  but i think i can  find one that is somewhat ready 