ocks  well  you're achieving  an increase in efficiency because  you're not selecting for all the root  traits  that you would be selecting for if you  were breeding a whole plant  and so you're separating the root traits  from the trays that make this apple very  very tasty and good  and also  other trays that belong to the top part  the scion  and  thereby gaining a huge amount of  efficiency so imagine  breeding  10 traits in the scion fruit shape fruit  color and  let's see what other things so that they  have bricks  so sweetness the crunchiness of the  apple  and all of those are controlled by many  many genes so imagine selecting for all  those traits  and then having to add  fire blight resistance in this in the  uh  and then resistance to phytophthora  resistance to pythium resistance to uh  rhizoctonia and uh and then the you know  complex uh  replant disease uh resistance and  and then dwarfing and dwarfing is two  two and a half genes  i know i know half a gene doesn't sound  right but  there are slightly modifiers and then  you start breeding  for the root system to absorb more  potassium be more efficient with calcium  absorption and and all these other  traits that are mounted up and if you  had to combine both cyan and rootstocks  together  you know it take more than 50 years yeah  or  a couple of medium plants  and  some very smart guy doing  uh whole  genome  selection  instead  the two  items are separated and then joined by  grafting  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu

